WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.369
Integer mistakes become especially important when they affect memory and buffer handling.

2
00:00:04.369 --> 00:00:11.286
Allocation sizes, copy lengths, indexes, offsets, string lengths, serialized record sizes, and capacity checks all depend on numeric calculations.

3
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:21.116
A wrong number in one of these paths can make later code believe it has more room than it really has, or less data than it needs.

4
00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:23.665
The first defense is naming the unit.

5
00:00:23.665 --> 00:00:30.582
Byte count, element count, character count, code point count, capacity, current length, remaining length, and serialized length are not

6
00:00:30.582 --> 00:00:30.946
interchangeable.

7
00:00:30.946 --> 00:00:35.679
A calculation that is correct for one unit may be incorrect for another.

8
00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:41.141
Code should make unit changes explicit instead of relying on nearby comments or caller memory.

9
00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:44.781
Multiplication is a frequent source of mistakes in size calculation.

10
00:00:44.781 --> 00:00:50.242
A request for count times element size can exceed the representable range before allocation begins.

11
00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:56.432
If the wrapped or truncated value is used, the program may allocate a smaller object than intended.

12
00:00:56.432 --> 00:01:00.801
The calculation must be checked before the result controls memory-sensitive behavior.

13
00:01:00.801 --> 00:01:03.349
Defensive maximums are part of size safety.

14
00:01:03.349 --> 00:01:11.723
Even if a value fits in the machine type, it may be too large for the application, service, tenant, device, or file format.

15
00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:15.364
Reasonable caps help protect reliability and make error handling predictable.

16
00:01:15.364 --> 00:01:19.004
They also give reviewers a concrete policy to compare against.

17
00:01:19.004 --> 00:01:24.830
Before allocation, copying, formatting, indexing, parsing, or serialization, verify the size math and the domain rule.

18
00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:33.567
The code should be able to answer what the value represents, what maximum is acceptable, whether any multiplication or addition was checked, and what

19
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.480
happens if the requested operation is too large.
